  ('fe-g00010', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-botany', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'A climber is exploring a lowland forest in Luzon and encounters a tree with buttress roots and a distinct reddish bark. The climber identifies it as Red Lauan. What is the scientific name and family of this tree?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Shorea negrosensis / Dipterocarpaceae"},{"id":"b","text":"Shorea contorta / Dipterocarpaceae"},{"id":"c","text":"Shorea polysperma / Dipterocarpaceae"},{"id":"d","text":"Shorea almon / Dipterocarpaceae"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'Shorea contorta, commonly known as Red Lauan, is a species within the Dipterocarpaceae family. It is characterized by its buttress roots and reddish bark, typical features of this tree in lowland forests of Luzon.',
   'Shorea contorta is correctly identified as Red Lauan due to its distinct reddish bark and buttress roots, which are adaptations for stability in tropical environments. Option a, Shorea negrosensis, is known as the ''Negros Red Lauan'' but is less common in Luzon. Option c, Shorea polysperma, is known as ''Tanguile'' and lacks the distinct reddish bark. Option d, Shorea almon, is known as ''Almon'' and does not typically have the same buttress root structure. The correct identification relies on the combination of bark color and root structure.',
   'Remember that Red Lauan (Shorea contorta) is identified by its reddish bark and buttress roots, common in Luzon lowland forests.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00011', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-botany', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'During a field study in a mangrove area, a forestry student measures a tree with pencil-like pneumatophores and a dense, leathery leaf structure. The student must correctly identify the tree and its family. What is the most likely classification?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Sonneratia alba / Lythraceae"},{"id":"b","text":"Rhizophora apiculata / Rhizophoraceae"},{"id":"c","text":"Avicennia marina / Acanthaceae"},{"id":"d","text":"Bruguiera gymnorhiza / Rhizophoraceae"}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Avicennia marina, belonging to the Acanthaceae family, is known for its pencil-like pneumatophores and leathery leaves, which are adaptations for survival in mangrove environments.',
   'Avicennia marina is correctly identified by its pencil-like pneumatophores and dense, leathery leaves, which help it survive in saline conditions. Option b, Sonneratia alba, has conical pneumatophores and is in the Lythraceae family. Option c, Rhizophora apiculata, has stilt roots, not pneumatophores, and is in the Rhizophoraceae family. Option d, Bruguiera gymnorhiza, also has knee roots, not pencil-like pneumatophores, and is in the Rhizophoraceae family. The key to identification is the specific root and leaf structure.',
   'Look for pencil-like pneumatophores and leathery leaves to identify Avicennia marina in mangrove areas.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00012', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-botany', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'In a biodiversity assessment in the Visayas, a forester records a hardwood tree notable for its straight bole, often used in construction, and classified within the Fabaceae family. The forester needs to identify this tree. Which tree fits this description?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Intsia bijuga / Fabaceae"},{"id":"b","text":"Vitex parviflora / Lamiaceae"},{"id":"c","text":"Pterocarpus indicus / Fabaceae"},{"id":"d","text":"Albizia saman / Fabaceae"}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Pterocarpus indicus, known as Narra, is a hardwood tree in the Fabaceae family, recognized for its straight bole and widespread use in construction.',
   'Pterocarpus indicus is correctly identified as it is a hardwood tree with a straight bole, commonly used in construction, and belongs to the Fabaceae family. Option a, Intsia bijuga, also in Fabaceae, is known as ''Ipil'' and has a more irregular bole. Option c, Vitex parviflora, is not in the Fabaceae family and is known as ''Molave''. Option d, Albizia saman, known as ''Rain Tree'', has a spreading canopy and is not typically used for construction. The straight bole and family classification are key identifiers for Pterocarpus indicus.',
   'Identify Pterocarpus indicus by its straight bole and use in construction, typical of Fabaceae hardwoods.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00013', 'forest-ecosystem', 'dendrology', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'What is the scientific name of the Narra tree, the national tree of the Philippines?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Gmelina arborea"},{"id":"b","text":"Shorea contorta"},{"id":"c","text":"Pterocarpus indicus"},{"id":"d","text":"Tectona grandis"}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Pterocarpus indicus is the correct scientific name for the Narra tree, which is recognized as the national tree of the Philippines. This species is known for its hardwood and is culturally significant in the country.',
   'Pterocarpus indicus is the correct answer because it is the scientific name for the Narra tree, which is the national tree of the Philippines. Gmelina arborea, Shorea contorta, and Tectona grandis are different species altogether. Gmelina arborea belongs to the family Lamiaceae and is commonly known as Yemane. Shorea contorta is a member of the Dipterocarpaceae family and is known locally as White Lauan. Tectona grandis, known as Teak, belongs to the family Lamiaceae. Therefore, only Pterocarpus indicus correctly identifies the Narra tree.',
   'Remember that the Narra tree, the national tree of the Philippines, is scientifically named Pterocarpus indicus.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00014', 'forest-ecosystem', 'dendrology', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'To which family does the tree species Hopea philippinensis belong?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Fabaceae"},{"id":"b","text":"Myrtaceae"},{"id":"c","text":"Rutaceae"},{"id":"d","text":"Dipterocarpaceae"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Hopea philippinensis belongs to the Dipterocarpaceae family, which is known for its tropical hardwood trees. This family is significant in the Philippines due to its ecological and economic importance.',
   'Hopea philippinensis is correctly classified under the Dipterocarpaceae family. This family includes many important timber trees in the Philippines. The other options are incorrect: Fabaceae is a family that includes legumes, Myrtaceae includes species like eucalyptus and guava, and Rutaceae is known for citrus plants. Therefore, only Dipterocarpaceae is the correct family for Hopea philippinensis.',
   'For tree species in the Philippines, remember that many hardwoods, including Hopea philippinensis, belong to the Dipterocarpaceae family.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00015', 'forest-ecosystem', 'dendrology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'What is the scientific name and family of Yakal, a common tree species in the Philippines used for high-quality timber?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Pterocarpus indicus / Fabaceae"},{"id":"b","text":"Swietenia macrophylla / Meliaceae"},{"id":"c","text":"Acacia mangium / Fabaceae"},{"id":"d","text":"Shorea astylosa / Dipterocarpaceae"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Shorea astylosa is commonly known as Yakal and belongs to the Dipterocarpaceae family. This species is highly valued for its durable and high-quality timber, making it a preferred choice for construction and furniture.',
   'Shorea astylosa, known as Yakal, is part of the Dipterocarpaceae family, which is renowned for its hardwood species. Option b, Pterocarpus indicus, is known as Narra and belongs to the Fabaceae family, which is incorrect for Yakal. Option c, Swietenia macrophylla, is Mahogany from the Meliaceae family, and option d, Acacia mangium, is also from the Fabaceae family, neither of which are related to Yakal.',
   'Remember that Yakal is a Dipterocarp, which is a key timber species in the Philippines.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00016', 'forest-ecosystem', 'dendrology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'Which of the following characteristics is true of the species Dipterocarpus grandiflorus, known locally as Apitong?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"It is typically found in montane forests."},{"id":"b","text":"It produces very light and soft wood."},{"id":"c","text":"It has winged seeds adapted for wind dispersal."},{"id":"d","text":"It belongs to the Meliaceae family."}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Dipterocarpus grandiflorus, or Apitong, is known for its winged seeds, which are adapted for wind dispersal. This adaptation helps the seeds travel away from the parent tree to reduce competition and increase survival chances.',
   'Option c is correct because Dipterocarpus grandiflorus has seeds with wings that facilitate wind dispersal. Option a is incorrect because Apitong is typically found in lowland dipterocarp forests, not montane forests. Option b is incorrect as Apitong wood is heavy and durable, not light and soft. Option d is incorrect because Apitong belongs to the Dipterocarpaceae family, not Meliaceae.',
   'Dipterocarps are known for their winged seeds, which is a key identifying feature.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00017', 'forest-ecosystem', 'dendrology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'What is the common name and family of the tree species Canarium ovatum, which is known for producing valuable nuts?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Talisay / Combretaceae"},{"id":"b","text":"Bitaog / Calophyllaceae"},{"id":"c","text":"Pili / Burseraceae"},{"id":"d","text":"Mango / Anacardiaceae"}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Canarium ovatum is commonly known as Pili and belongs to the Burseraceae family. It is valued for its edible nuts, which are a significant agricultural product in the Philippines.',
   'The correct answer is c, as Canarium ovatum is known as Pili and is part of the Burseraceae family. Option a, Talisay, belongs to the Combretaceae family. Option b, Bitaog, is from the Calophyllaceae family. Option d, Mango, belongs to the Anacardiaceae family. None of these options match the characteristics of Canarium ovatum.',
   'Remember that Pili nuts come from Canarium ovatum, part of the Burseraceae family.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00018', 'forest-ecosystem', 'dendrology', 'Medium', 'practice',
