  ('fe-g00019', 'forest-ecosystem', 'dendrology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'Which characteristic helps distinguish the tree species Tectona philippinensis from other Philippine hardwoods?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"It features compound leaves typical of many dipterocarps."},{"id":"b","text":"It produces large, solitary flowers."},{"id":"c","text":"It belongs to the Meliaceae family."},{"id":"d","text":"It has a distinctive flaky bark."}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Tectona philippinensis, or Philippine teak, is distinguished by its flaky bark, which is a unique characteristic among Philippine hardwoods. This feature helps in identifying the species in the field.',
   'Option b is correct because Tectona philippinensis has a distinctive flaky bark. Option a is incorrect as compound leaves are not a distinguishing feature of Tectona philippinensis. Option c is incorrect because while it does produce flowers, they are not large and solitary. Option d is incorrect as Tectona belongs to the Lamiaceae family, not Meliaceae.',
   'Look for the flaky bark to identify Tectona philippinensis among hardwoods.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00020', 'forest-ecosystem', 'dendrology', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'In the context of Dendrology, a field forester encounters a tree with the following characteristics: alternate leaf arrangement, bipinnate leaves, and a cylindrical pod fruit. Based on these characteristics, which of the following tree species is being described?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Leucaena leucocephala / Fabaceae"},{"id":"b","text":"Pterocarpus indicus / Fabaceae"},{"id":"c","text":"Acacia mangium / Fabaceae"},{"id":"d","text":"Swietenia macrophylla / Meliaceae"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'Leucaena leucocephala is known for its bipinnate leaves, alternate leaf arrangement, and cylindrical pod fruit, which matches the described characteristics. It is a member of the Fabaceae family, commonly found in the Philippines.',
   'Leucaena leucocephala is correctly identified because it has bipinnate leaves, which are a key feature of this species, along with alternate leaf arrangement and cylindrical pods. Pterocarpus indicus, although in the same family, has different leaf and fruit characteristics, typically with pinnate leaves and samara-type fruits. Acacia mangium, also in Fabaceae, has bipinnate leaves but often has a different pod shape. Swietenia macrophylla, from the Meliaceae family, has pinnate leaves and capsule fruits, not matching the description.',
   'Remember that bipinnate leaves and cylindrical pods are indicative of Leucaena leucocephala.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00021', 'forest-ecosystem', 'dendrology', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'A forestry student is asked to identify a tree species that has simple, alternate, and entire leaves with a milky sap. Given these characteristics, which species does the student correctly identify as common in the Philippines?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Artocarpus heterophyllus / Moraceae"},{"id":"b","text":"Ficus nota / Moraceae"},{"id":"c","text":"Mangifera indica / Anacardiaceae"},{"id":"d","text":"Palaquium ellipticum / Sapotaceae"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'Ficus nota is characterized by simple, alternate, entire leaves, and a milky sap, which are the features described. It belongs to the Moraceae family and is common in the Philippines.',
   'Ficus nota is the correct choice because it has simple, alternate, entire leaves and produces a milky sap, fitting the description given. Artocarpus heterophyllus, while also in the Moraceae family, typically has compound leaves and is known for its large fruit. Mangifera indica, from the Anacardiaceae family, has simple leaves but lacks the milky sap characteristic. Palaquium ellipticum, from the Sapotaceae family, has milky sap but its leaves are not simple and alternate as described.',
   'Identify Ficus species by their milky sap and simple, alternate leaves.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00022', 'forest-ecosystem', 'dendrology', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'During a field survey in a secondary forest in the Philippines, a botanist records the presence of a tree species with opposite, compound leaves, and small white fragrant flowers in clusters. Which species do the botanist''s observations most likely refer to?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Cinnamomum mercadoi / Lauraceae"},{"id":"b","text":"Vitex parviflora / Lamiaceae"},{"id":"c","text":"Spathodea campanulata / Bignoniaceae"},{"id":"d","text":"Santalum album / Santalaceae"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'Vitex parviflora is known for its opposite, compound leaves and small white fragrant flowers in clusters, which matches the botanist''s observations. It belongs to the Lamiaceae family.',
   'Vitex parviflora is correctly identified due to its opposite, compound leaves and small white fragrant flowers, fitting the description. Cinnamomum mercadoi, from the Lauraceae family, typically has simple leaves and lacks the described flower characteristics. Spathodea campanulata, in the Bignoniaceae family, has large, showy flowers and simple leaves, not matching the description. Santalum album, from the Santalaceae family, has simple leaves and different floral characteristics.',
   'Look for opposite, compound leaves and small white flowers to identify Vitex parviflora.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00023', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-ecology', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'In forest ecology, what term describes the complex interaction between living organisms and their physical environment in a forest?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Forest ecosystem"},{"id":"b","text":"Forest succession"},{"id":"c","text":"Forest canopy"},{"id":"d","text":"Forest floor"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'The term ''forest ecosystem'' refers to the complex interactions between living organisms, such as plants, animals, and microorganisms, and their physical environment within a forest. This includes the biotic and abiotic components that interact as a system.',
   'A forest ecosystem encompasses all the living organisms (biotic components) like trees, animals, and microorganisms, and non-living (abiotic components) such as soil, water, and climate, interacting as a system. Option b, ''forest succession,'' refers to the natural process of change in species structure over time. Option c, ''forest canopy,'' describes the upper layer of trees and foliage in a forest. Option d, ''forest floor,'' refers to the bottom layer of the forest, consisting of soil, fallen leaves, and decomposing organic matter.',
   'Think of an ecosystem as the ''whole picture'' of interactions in a forest, including both living and non-living components.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00024', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-ecology', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'What is the term for the maximum population size of a species that an environment can sustain indefinitely without being degraded?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Carrying capacity"},{"id":"b","text":"Population density"},{"id":"c","text":"Biotic potential"},{"id":"d","text":"Ecological footprint"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'Carrying capacity is the term used to describe the maximum number of individuals of a particular species that an environment can support sustainably. It considers the availability of resources such as food, water, and shelter, as well as the impact of the species on the environment.',
   'The correct answer is ''carrying capacity'' because it specifically refers to the ability of an environment to support a species over the long term without environmental degradation. ''Population density'' refers to the number of individuals per unit area, which does not account for sustainability. ''Biotic potential'' is the maximum reproductive capacity of a species under ideal conditions, not considering environmental limits. ''Ecological footprint'' measures the environmental impact of an individual or population, not the environment''s capacity to support them. Carrying capacity is a key concept in forest ecology, ensuring that populations remain in balance with their ecosystems.',
   'Remember that ''carrying capacity'' is about sustainability and long-term support of a population by the environment.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00025', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-ecology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In a mangrove forest, which process describes the transition from a community dominated by pioneer species to one with more complex species interactions and structure?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Primary succession"},{"id":"b","text":"Climax community formation"},{"id":"c","text":"Anthropogenic disturbance recovery"},{"id":"d","text":"Secondary succession"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Secondary succession is the process that occurs in an area where a disturbance has destroyed a community without destroying the soil. In mangrove forests, this process involves the transition from pioneer species to more complex species interactions and structures.',
   'Secondary succession is the correct answer because it refers to the ecological succession that occurs on pre-existing soil after the primary succession has been disrupted or destroyed due to a disturbance. In mangrove forests, disturbances such as storms or human activities can lead to secondary succession. Primary succession (option a) occurs on newly formed or exposed substrates without soil, such as lava flows. Climax community formation (option c) is the final stage of succession, not the process itself. Anthropogenic disturbance recovery (option d) is not a recognized ecological succession process.',
   'Remember that secondary succession involves soil that remains intact after a disturbance.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00026', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-ecology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'Which of the following is a characteristic of the leaf litter layer in tropical rainforests that affects nutrient cycling?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Low pH slowing down microbial activity"},{"id":"b","text":"High moisture content leading to rapid decomposition"},{"id":"c","text":"High clay content affecting soil permeability"},{"id":"d","text":"Presence of evergreen leaf species reducing litter fall"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'In tropical rainforests, the leaf litter layer has high moisture content, which accelerates the decomposition process and affects nutrient cycling by rapidly returning nutrients to the soil.',
   'The correct answer is a, high moisture content leading to rapid decomposition, because tropical rainforests have a warm and humid climate that promotes rapid decomposition of organic matter. This rapid decomposition is crucial for nutrient cycling. Low pH (option b) would slow down microbial activity, not a characteristic of tropical rainforest leaf litter. High clay content (option c) affects soil permeability, not directly related to leaf litter. Evergreen leaf species (option d) would reduce litter fall, but this does not describe the characteristic of the leaf litter layer affecting nutrient cycling.',
   'In tropical climates, think of moisture and heat as accelerators of decomposition.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00027', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-ecology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'Considering forest edge effects, which factor typically increases at the boundary between a forest and an open area?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Light intensity"},{"id":"b","text":"Soil fertility"},{"id":"c","text":"Moisture retention"},{"id":"d","text":"Biodiversity of interior species"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'At the boundary between a forest and an open area, light intensity typically increases due to the absence of a canopy that would otherwise block sunlight.',
   'The correct answer is b, light intensity, because at the forest edge, the absence of a dense canopy allows more sunlight to penetrate, increasing light intensity. Soil fertility (option a) does not typically increase at the edge; it may decrease due to erosion. Moisture retention (option c) is often lower at the edge due to increased exposure to wind and sun. Biodiversity of interior species (option d) usually decreases at the edge due to habitat fragmentation and edge effects.',
   'Edges are brighter due to less canopy cover, increasing light exposure.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00028', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-ecology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In the context of forest ecology, what role do mycorrhizal fungi primarily play in a forest ecosystem?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Facilitate nutrient uptake by plant roots"},{"id":"b","text":"Fix atmospheric nitrogen"},{"id":"c","text":"Decompose organic matter"},{"id":"d","text":"Pollinate flowering plants"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'Mycorrhizal fungi primarily facilitate nutrient uptake by plant roots, forming symbiotic relationships that enhance the plant''s ability to absorb water and nutrients, particularly phosphorus.',
   'The correct answer is b, facilitate nutrient uptake by plant roots, because mycorrhizal fungi form symbiotic associations with plant roots, enhancing nutrient and water absorption. Option a, fixing atmospheric nitrogen, is a function of certain bacteria, not mycorrhizal fungi. Option c, decomposing organic matter, is primarily the role of saprophytic fungi and bacteria. Option d, pollinating flowering plants, is a function of animals like bees and birds, not fungi.',
   'Think of mycorrhizal fungi as nutrient facilitators for plants.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00029', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-ecology', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'In a study of the carbon sequestration potential of different forest types in the Philippines, a researcher finds that the rate of carbon uptake in a dipterocarp forest is significantly lower than expected. Considering the concepts of net primary productivity (NPP) and respiration, what could be a plausible ecological explanation for this observation?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Increased soil fertility enhancing carbon sequestration potential."},{"id":"b","text":"Lower rates of heterotrophic respiration leading to higher carbon uptake."},{"id":"c","text":"Decreased leaf litter decomposition diminishing nutrient cycling."},{"id":"d","text":"High rates of autotrophic respiration reducing net primary productivity."}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'High rates of autotrophic respiration can significantly reduce the net primary productivity (NPP) of a forest. In a dipterocarp forest, if the autotrophic respiration is high, it means that a large portion of the carbon fixed during photosynthesis is being used for plant metabolic processes, leaving less carbon available for sequestration.',
   'The correct answer is ''a'' because autotrophic respiration refers to the respiration carried out by plants themselves, which consumes part of the carbon fixed during photosynthesis. If this respiration rate is high, it reduces the net primary productivity, which is the amount of carbon uptake after accounting for respiration. Option ''b'' is incorrect because increased soil fertility would likely enhance, not reduce, carbon sequestration. Option ''c'' is incorrect because lower heterotrophic respiration would not directly explain lower carbon uptake; it affects decomposition rather than NPP. Option ''d'' is incorrect because decreased leaf litter decomposition would likely lead to nutrient limitations, not directly affecting carbon uptake rates.',
   'Remember that high respiration rates in plants can reduce the net carbon gain, impacting overall carbon sequestration.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00030', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-ecology', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'In a tropical forest undergoing secondary succession, a forester observes an increase in species richness over time. According to the intermediate disturbance hypothesis, what is a key factor that could explain this pattern?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Low disturbance frequency allows dominant species to outcompete others, reducing diversity."},{"id":"b","text":"Frequent, moderate disturbances create opportunities for both colonizing and competitive species."},{"id":"c","text":"High disturbance frequency prevents establishment of a stable community, maintaining low diversity."},{"id":"d","text":"Complete absence of disturbance leads to maximum species richness through succession climax."}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'The intermediate disturbance hypothesis suggests that species richness is highest at intermediate levels of disturbance. Frequent, moderate disturbances prevent any single species from dominating, allowing both colonizing and competitive species to coexist, thereby increasing diversity.',
   'The correct answer is ''a'' because moderate disturbances create a dynamic environment where no single species can dominate, allowing for a mix of early successional species and more competitive, later successional species. Option ''b'' is incorrect because low disturbance frequency allows dominant species to monopolize resources, reducing diversity. Option ''c'' is incorrect because high disturbance frequency disrupts the community too often for stable communities to establish, maintaining low diversity. Option ''d'' is incorrect because the absence of disturbance typically leads to competitive exclusion, where a few species dominate, reducing overall diversity.',
   'Link moderate disturbances with increased diversity; too little or too much disturbance reduces species richness.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00031', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-soils', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'What is the primary role of soil organic matter in forest soils?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Improving soil structure and fertility"},{"id":"b","text":"Decreasing soil acidity significantly"},{"id":"c","text":"Increasing the mineral content of rocks"},{"id":"d","text":"Preventing all forms of soil erosion"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'Soil organic matter is crucial for enhancing soil structure by binding soil particles into aggregates, which improves aeration and water infiltration. It also contributes to soil fertility by providing essential nutrients as it decomposes.',
   'The correct option, a, is right because soil organic matter improves soil structure by helping to form stable aggregates, which enhance water retention and aeration. It also increases soil fertility by slowly releasing nutrients as it decomposes. Option b is incorrect because while organic matter can buffer soil acidity, it does not significantly decrease it. Option c is incorrect as organic matter does not increase the mineral content of rocks; minerals are derived from parent material. Option d is incorrect because organic matter alone cannot prevent all forms of soil erosion; it is part of a broader strategy including vegetation cover and land management practices.',
   'Remember that organic matter is key to both structure and fertility; think of it as the ''glue and food'' of the soil.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00032', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-soils', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'Which soil horizon is primarily composed of organic material and is found at the surface of most forest soils?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"B horizon"},{"id":"b","text":"C horizon"},{"id":"c","text":"R horizon"},{"id":"d","text":"O horizon"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'The O horizon is the topmost layer of soil, rich in organic material such as decomposed leaves, plants, and other organic debris, making it distinct from other soil horizons.',
   'The O horizon is primarily composed of organic material and is found at the surface of forest soils, making option a correct. This layer is rich in decomposed leaves, twigs, and other plant material. Option b, the B horizon, is a subsurface layer that accumulates minerals leached from above. Option c, the C horizon, consists of weathered parent material and lacks significant organic content. Option d, the R horizon, is unweathered bedrock and contains no organic material. The O horizon is critical for nutrient cycling and supporting forest ecosystems.',
   'Think ''O for Organic'' when identifying the surface layer rich in decomposed material.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00033', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-soils', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In assessing soil texture in a forest soil survey, what is the percentage of sand if the soil sample contains 20% clay, 30% silt, and the rest is sand?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"50%"},{"id":"b","text":"40%"},{"id":"c","text":"60%"},{"id":"d","text":"30%"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'The percentage of sand is calculated by subtracting the sum of clay and silt percentages from 100%. Since the soil sample contains 20% clay and 30% silt, the remaining 50% is sand.',
   'To find the percentage of sand, you subtract the percentages of clay and silt from 100%. The calculation is as follows: 100% - 20% (clay) - 30% (silt) = 50% sand. Option b (40%) and option d (30%) are incorrect because they do not account for the correct subtraction from 100%. Option c (60%) is incorrect as it overestimates the sand content by not properly subtracting the clay and silt percentages.',
   'Remember that soil texture percentages always add up to 100%.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00034', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-soils', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'Which of the following is a key indicator of soil fertility in forest ecosystems?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Soil texture"},{"id":"b","text":"Soil temperature"},{"id":"c","text":"Cation exchange capacity"},{"id":"d","text":"Soil color"}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Cation exchange capacity (CEC) is a key indicator of soil fertility because it measures the soil''s ability to hold and exchange essential nutrients, which is crucial for plant growth.',
   'Cation exchange capacity (CEC) is a measure of how well soil can retain and supply cations to plant roots. It is a direct indicator of soil fertility. Option a (Soil texture) affects drainage and aeration but not directly fertility. Option c (Soil temperature) influences biological activity but is not a direct fertility measure. Option d (Soil color) can indicate organic matter content but is not a direct measure of fertility.',
   'CEC is crucial for understanding nutrient availability in soils.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00035', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-soils', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'A forest soil sample with a pH of 5.5 is considered to be:',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Strongly acidic"},{"id":"b","text":"Moderately acidic"},{"id":"c","text":"Neutral"},{"id":"d","text":"Slightly alkaline"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'A pH of 5.5 is considered moderately acidic. Soil pH is a measure of acidity or alkalinity, and a pH below 7 is acidic.',
   'Soil pH is classified as follows: strongly acidic (pH < 5.0), moderately acidic (pH 5.0-6.0), neutral (pH 7.0), and slightly alkaline (pH > 7.0). Therefore, a pH of 5.5 falls within the moderately acidic range. Option a (strongly acidic) is incorrect because it applies to pH values below 5.0. Option c (neutral) is incorrect because neutral pH is 7.0. Option d (slightly alkaline) is incorrect as it applies to pH values above 7.0.',
   'Remember that pH 7 is neutral; below 7 is acidic, above 7 is alkaline.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00036', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-soils', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'What is the effect of high soil compaction on root growth in forest soils?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Enhanced root penetration due to increased density"},{"id":"b","text":"Neutral effect on root growth"},{"id":"c","text":"Increased nutrient uptake due to closer proximity of soil particles"},{"id":"d","text":"Reduced root penetration and stunted growth"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'High soil compaction reduces root penetration and stunts growth because it increases soil density, making it difficult for roots to grow and access nutrients and water.',
   'Compaction increases soil density, reducing pore space, which limits root growth and access to air and water. Option a (enhanced root penetration) is incorrect because compaction restricts root growth. Option c (neutral effect) is incorrect because compaction negatively impacts root growth. Option d (increased nutrient uptake) is incorrect because compaction limits nutrient access due to reduced root growth.',
   'Compacted soil restricts root growth due to reduced pore space.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00037', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-soils', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'Which soil property is primarily responsible for water retention in forest soils?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Sand content"},{"id":"b","text":"Gravel content"},{"id":"c","text":"Organic matter content"},{"id":"d","text":"Clay content"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Clay content is primarily responsible for water retention in forest soils because clay particles are small and have a large surface area, which helps retain water.',
   'Clay particles are tiny and have a high surface area-to-volume ratio, allowing them to hold water effectively. Option a (sand content) is incorrect because sand has large particles and drains water quickly. Option c (gravel content) is incorrect because gravel also drains water quickly due to large particle size. Option d (organic matter content) does help with water retention but is not as significant as clay content in terms of primary responsibility.',
   'Clay''s small particles and high surface area make it excellent for water retention.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00038', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-soils', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'A forester is conducting a soil survey in a dipterocarp forest. The soil has a bulk density of 1.3 g/cm³ and a porosity of 50%. How much water (in cm³) can be retained in a 1 cm³ sample of this soil when it is fully saturated?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"0.65 cm³"},{"id":"b","text":"0.50 cm³"},{"id":"c","text":"0.35 cm³"},{"id":"d","text":"0.25 cm³"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'The porosity of the soil indicates the volume of voids or spaces in the soil, which can be filled with water when the soil is saturated. With a porosity of 50%, it means that 50% of the soil volume is voids that can hold water. Therefore, in a 1 cm³ sample, 0.50 cm³ can be filled with water when fully saturated.',
   'The porosity of a soil is the ratio of the volume of voids to the total volume of the soil. Given a porosity of 50%, this means that 0.50 of every 1 cm³ of soil is void space that can be filled with water. Thus, when the soil is fully saturated, it can retain 0.50 cm³ of water. Option a (0.65 cm³) exceeds the total volume of the soil sample, option c (0.35 cm³) and option d (0.25 cm³) are less than the porosity, indicating less than full saturation.',
   'Remember that porosity directly indicates the volume of water a soil can hold when fully saturated.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00039', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-soils', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'In the context of forest soils, Cation Exchange Capacity (CEC) is an important measure. Which statement best describes what CEC indicates about a forest soil?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"The ability of soil to exchange cations with plant roots, reflecting nutrient availability."},{"id":"b","text":"The rate at which soil organic matter decomposes, influencing soil fertility."},{"id":"c","text":"The soil''s capacity to retain water, thereby affecting drought resistance."},{"id":"d","text":"The proportion of soil particles that are clay, influencing soil texture."}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'Cation Exchange Capacity (CEC) is a measure of how well soil can retain and exchange cations, which are positively charged ions. This is crucial for nutrient availability to plants, as many essential nutrients are cations.',
   'CEC is a critical property of soil that affects its ability to supply nutrients to plants. It refers to the total capacity of a soil to hold exchangeable cations. Option b refers to organic matter decomposition, which is not directly related to CEC. Option c describes water retention, which is related to soil porosity, not CEC. Option d refers to soil texture, which affects physical properties but not CEC directly.',
   'CEC is about nutrient exchange; think of it as the soil''s ''nutrient bank'' for cations.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00040', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-soils', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'In a lowland forest in the Philippines, a soil sample shows a high rate of phosphorus fixation. Considering the characteristics of tropical soils, which management practice would best increase phosphorus availability to plants?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Using phosphate-solubilizing microorganisms to release bound phosphorus."},{"id":"b","text":"Incorporating lime to increase soil pH."},{"id":"c","text":"Applying organic matter to improve soil structure."},{"id":"d","text":"Reducing irrigation to limit phosphorus leaching."}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'Phosphate-solubilizing microorganisms can release phosphorus that is bound in the soil, making it available for plant uptake. This is particularly useful in tropical soils where phosphorus fixation is high.',
   'Tropical soils often fix phosphorus, making it unavailable to plants. Using phosphate-solubilizing microorganisms can help release this bound phosphorus. Option a (lime) affects soil pH but may not directly release bound phosphorus. Option b (organic matter) improves structure but not necessarily phosphorus availability. Option d (reducing irrigation) does not address phosphorus fixation and could exacerbate nutrient availability issues.',
   'For phosphorus issues, think of biological solutions like microorganisms to unlock bound nutrients.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00041', 'forest-ecosystem', 'tree-physiology', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'What is the primary function of xylem in trees?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Transporting water from roots to leaves."},{"id":"b","text":"Photosynthesis."},{"id":"c","text":"Releasing oxygen into the atmosphere."},{"id":"d","text":"Storing carbohydrates."}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'The xylem is a type of vascular tissue in trees that is responsible for the transport of water and dissolved minerals from the roots to the leaves. This process is essential for maintaining the tree''s water balance and supporting photosynthesis.',
   'The primary function of xylem is to transport water and minerals from the roots to the leaves, which is essential for photosynthesis and nutrient distribution. Option b, photosynthesis, is incorrect because it is a process that occurs in the chloroplasts of leaves, not a function of xylem. Option c, releasing oxygen, is a result of photosynthesis, not a function of xylem. Option d, storing carbohydrates, is a function of phloem, not xylem. The xylem''s structure, composed of vessels and tracheids, facilitates this upward movement of water through capillary action and transpiration pull.',
   'Remember that xylem is associated with water transport, while phloem is linked to nutrient transport.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00042', 'forest-ecosystem', 'tree-physiology', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'In tree physiology, what is the role of stomata?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Transporting nutrients throughout the tree."},{"id":"b","text":"Anchoring trees into the soil."},{"id":"c","text":"Facilitating gas exchange and transpiration."},{"id":"d","text":"Producing seeds and fruits."}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Stomata are small openings on the surface of leaves that allow for gas exchange and transpiration. They enable the intake of carbon dioxide for photosynthesis and the release of oxygen and water vapor.',
   'Stomata play a crucial role in facilitating gas exchange and transpiration, allowing carbon dioxide to enter the leaf for photosynthesis and releasing oxygen and water vapor. Option a, transporting nutrients, is incorrect because this is the role of the vascular system (xylem and phloem). Option c, anchoring trees, is the function of roots. Option d, producing seeds and fruits, is related to the reproductive structures of the plant, not stomata. The opening and closing of stomata are regulated by guard cells, which respond to environmental conditions to optimize gas exchange and water retention.',
   'Link stomata with gas exchange and water regulation in leaves.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00043', 'forest-ecosystem', 'tree-physiology', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'Which of the following plant hormones is primarily responsible for promoting cell elongation in trees?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Cytokinin"},{"id":"b","text":"Gibberellin"},{"id":"c","text":"Abscisic acid"},{"id":"d","text":"Auxin"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Auxin is a plant hormone that plays a key role in promoting cell elongation, especially in the shoots and roots of plants. It is crucial for the growth and development of trees.',
   'Auxin is primarily responsible for promoting cell elongation, which is essential for plant growth. It influences the elongation of cells in the stem and roots, allowing the plant to grow towards light (phototropism) or gravity (gravitropism). Option b, cytokinin, is involved in cell division and differentiation. Option c, gibberellin, also promotes growth but is more associated with stem elongation and seed germination. Option d, abscisic acid, is involved in stress responses and inhibiting growth. Auxin''s role in cell elongation is vital for directional growth and the overall development of the plant.',
   'Associate auxin with cell elongation and directional growth in plants.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00044', 'forest-ecosystem', 'tree-physiology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'When a tree undergoes photosynthesis, which component of the process directly involves chlorophyll capturing light energy?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Light-independent reactions"},{"id":"b","text":"Light-dependent reactions"},{"id":"c","text":"Transpiration"},{"id":"d","text":"Respiration"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'The light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis occur in the thylakoid membranes of the chloroplasts, where chlorophyll absorbs light energy. This energy is then used to split water molecules and generate ATP and NADPH, which are essential for the subsequent light-independent reactions.',
   'In photosynthesis, the light-dependent reactions are the first phase where chlorophyll captures light energy. This occurs in the thylakoid membranes of chloroplasts. The absorbed light energy is used to split water molecules (photolysis), releasing oxygen and producing ATP and NADPH. Option b, light-independent reactions, occur in the stroma and do not directly involve light capture. Option c, transpiration, is the process of water movement through a plant and its evaporation from aerial parts, not related to light capture. Option d, respiration, is a separate process where glucose is broken down to release energy.',
   'Remember that chlorophyll captures light during the light-dependent reactions.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00045', 'forest-ecosystem', 'tree-physiology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In tree physiology, what is the primary purpose of phloem tissue?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Transporting water and minerals from roots to leaves"},{"id":"b","text":"Storing starch in roots and stems"},{"id":"c","text":"Conducting nutrients produced in leaves to other parts of the plant"},{"id":"d","text":"Providing structural support to the tree"}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Phloem tissue is responsible for transporting the products of photosynthesis, mainly sugars, from the leaves where they are produced to other parts of the plant that need energy or storage.',
   'Phloem tissue conducts nutrients, primarily in the form of sugars, from the leaves to other parts of the plant, which is why option b is correct. Option a describes the function of xylem, which transports water and minerals. Option c, storing starch, is not a primary function of phloem but rather occurs in storage tissues. Option d, providing structural support, is a function of xylem and other supportive tissues, not phloem.',
   'Phloem flows food; remember ''phloem'' and ''food'' both start with ''f''.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00046', 'forest-ecosystem', 'tree-physiology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'A forester measures the rate of transpiration in a Benguet pine. Which factor is most likely to increase this rate?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"High humidity"},{"id":"b","text":"Strong winds"},{"id":"c","text":"Low air temperature"},{"id":"d","text":"Reduced sunlight"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'Strong winds increase the rate of transpiration by removing the boundary layer of humid air around the leaf surface, enhancing water vapor loss from the stomata.',
   'Transpiration is the process of water vapor loss from plant leaves. Strong winds (option c) remove the humid air layer near the leaf surface, increasing the rate of transpiration. High humidity (option a) reduces transpiration as the air is already saturated with moisture. Low air temperature (option b) reduces transpiration as it slows down evaporation. Reduced sunlight (option d) decreases the opening of stomata, thereby reducing transpiration.',
   'Wind increases transpiration by removing humid air around leaves.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00047', 'forest-ecosystem', 'tree-physiology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'Which tree physiological process is directly affected by a deficiency of magnesium in the soil?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Photosynthesis"},{"id":"b","text":"Nitrogen fixation"},{"id":"c","text":"Water uptake"},{"id":"d","text":"Starch storage"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'Magnesium is a central component of the chlorophyll molecule, which is essential for photosynthesis. A deficiency in magnesium directly affects the plant''s ability to perform photosynthesis efficiently.',
   'Magnesium is crucial for photosynthesis because it is a core component of chlorophyll. Without adequate magnesium, chlorophyll production is impaired, reducing photosynthesis (option b). Nitrogen fixation (option a) primarily involves nitrogenase enzymes and is not directly related to magnesium. Water uptake (option c) is influenced by root function and soil moisture, not magnesium. Starch storage (option d) is a result of photosynthesis but not directly affected by magnesium deficiency.',
   'Magnesium is essential for chlorophyll, which is key to photosynthesis.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00048', 'forest-ecosystem', 'tree-physiology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'During which time of day is the rate of photosynthesis typically highest in tropical tree species?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Early morning, around sunrise"},{"id":"b","text":"Late afternoon, before sunset"},{"id":"c","text":"Night time"},{"id":"d","text":"Midday, around noon"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Photosynthesis rates are typically highest around midday when sunlight intensity is at its peak, providing the maximum energy for the light-dependent reactions.',
   'Photosynthesis requires light, and its rate is highest when light intensity is greatest, which is around noon (option b) in tropical regions. Early morning (option a) and late afternoon (option c) have lower light intensities. Night time (option d) lacks sunlight, so photosynthesis does not occur.',
   'Photosynthesis peaks at midday due to maximum sunlight.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00049', 'forest-ecosystem', 'tree-physiology', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'A mahogany tree in a plantation exhibits a delayed response to pruning. Which plant hormone is most likely involved in signaling the tree to heal and regrow new shoots following pruning?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Ethylene"},{"id":"b","text":"Gibberellin"},{"id":"c","text":"Abscisic acid"},{"id":"d","text":"Cytokinin"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Cytokinin is a plant hormone that promotes cell division and shoot formation, which are crucial processes for healing and regrowth after pruning.',
   'Cytokinin is the correct answer because it is involved in promoting cell division and the growth of new shoots, which are essential for the tree''s recovery and regrowth after pruning. Ethylene is more associated with fruit ripening and leaf abscission, not regrowth. Gibberellin primarily promotes stem elongation and seed germination, while abscisic acid is involved in stress responses and dormancy, not active growth.',
   'Remember that cytokinins are key for cell division and shoot development, especially after pruning.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00050', 'forest-ecosystem', 'tree-physiology', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'A forester assesses the drought tolerance of a dipterocarp species by examining its root system. What is the primary physiological adaptation in the roots that might enhance its drought resistance?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Higher stomatal density in aerial parts"},{"id":"b","text":"Increased root surface area for water absorption"},{"id":"c","text":"Production of thicker bark"},{"id":"d","text":"Reduction in leaf size"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'Increased root surface area allows for more efficient water absorption, which is crucial for drought resistance.',
   'The correct answer is increased root surface area for water absorption because it directly enhances the plant''s ability to take up water from the soil, which is vital during drought conditions. Higher stomatal density would actually increase water loss through transpiration. Thicker bark and reduced leaf size are more related to protection and reducing water loss, respectively, but do not directly enhance water uptake like increased root surface area does.',
   'Think of roots as the primary means for water uptake; more surface area means better absorption.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00051', 'forest-ecosystem', 'tree-physiology', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'In the context of plant water relations, a forester wants to estimate the relative water content (RWC) of a tree sample. If the fresh weight of a leaf is 12 g, its dry weight is 4 g, and its turgid weight is 16 g, what is the RWC of the leaf?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"75%"},{"id":"b","text":"50%"},{"id":"c","text":"80%"},{"id":"d","text":"100%"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'The relative water content (RWC) is calculated using the formula: RWC = [(Fresh Weight - Dry Weight) / (Turgid Weight - Dry Weight)] x 100%.',
   'To find the RWC, use the formula: RWC = [(Fresh Weight - Dry Weight) / (Turgid Weight - Dry Weight)] x 100%. Plugging in the values: RWC = [(12 g - 4 g) / (16 g - 4 g)] x 100% = (8 g / 12 g) x 100% = 66.67%, which rounds to 75%. The other options are incorrect because they either miscalculate the formula or do not apply it correctly.',
   'Use the RWC formula: (Fresh - Dry) / (Turgid - Dry) x 100% to quickly estimate water content.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00052', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-entomology', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'What is the primary role of insects like the red slug caterpillar in forest ecosystems?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Herbivory"},{"id":"b","text":"Pollination"},{"id":"c","text":"Decomposition"},{"id":"d","text":"Seed dispersal"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'The primary role of insects like the red slug caterpillar in forest ecosystems is herbivory. These insects feed on plant material, specifically leaves, which can influence plant health and forest dynamics.',
   'The correct answer is herbivory because the red slug caterpillar feeds on the leaves of plants, impacting their growth and survival. Option a, pollination, is incorrect because red slug caterpillars do not assist in transferring pollen between flowers. Option b, decomposition, is incorrect as these caterpillars do not break down dead organic matter. Option c, seed dispersal, is incorrect because they do not aid in spreading seeds. Herbivory involves insects consuming plant parts, which can regulate plant populations and affect forest structure.',
   'Remember that caterpillars are primarily herbivores, feeding on plant leaves.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00053', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-entomology', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'In forest entomology, which insect is commonly known as a pest that defoliates dipterocarp trees in the Philippines?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Red slug caterpillar"},{"id":"b","text":"Bagworm moth"},{"id":"c","text":"Asian longhorn beetle"},{"id":"d","text":"Emerald ash borer"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'The red slug caterpillar is known as a pest that defoliates dipterocarp trees in the Philippines. It feeds on the leaves, causing significant damage to these trees.',
   'The red slug caterpillar is the correct answer because it is notorious for defoliating dipterocarp trees, which are prevalent in Philippine forests. Option a, bagworm moth, is incorrect as it typically feeds on coniferous trees and shrubs. Option b, Asian longhorn beetle, is incorrect because it primarily targets hardwood trees like maples and elms. Option d, emerald ash borer, is incorrect as it specifically targets ash trees. The red slug caterpillar''s feeding habits can lead to reduced tree growth and increased susceptibility to other stressors.',
   'Associate the red slug caterpillar with defoliation of dipterocarp trees in the Philippines.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00054', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-entomology', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'Which term describes the process by which insects control plant populations by feeding on them in a forest ecosystem?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Parasitism"},{"id":"b","text":"Predation"},{"id":"c","text":"Symbiosis"},{"id":"d","text":"Herbivory"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Herbivory describes the process by which insects control plant populations by feeding on them. This interaction is crucial in shaping plant community structures and dynamics.',
   'Herbivory is the correct term because it involves insects feeding on plants, thus controlling their populations. Option a, parasitism, is incorrect as it involves one organism benefiting at the expense of another, typically without immediate death. Option c, predation, is incorrect because it involves an organism killing and consuming another organism. Option d, symbiosis, is incorrect as it refers to a close and often long-term interaction between different biological species. Herbivory can limit plant growth and reproduction, influencing forest composition.',
   'Link herbivory with insects feeding on plants, affecting their populations.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00055', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-entomology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'Which insect is known for forming galls on Philippine forest trees and can influence tree growth negatively?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Termite (Isoptera order)"},{"id":"b","text":"Gall wasp (Cynipidae family)"},{"id":"c","text":"Moth caterpillar (Lepidoptera order)"},{"id":"d","text":"Bark beetle (Scolytinae subfamily)"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'Gall wasps, belonging to the Cynipidae family, are known for inducing the formation of galls on trees, which can disrupt normal growth and nutrient distribution, negatively affecting the tree''s health.',
   'Gall wasps (Cynipidae family) induce the formation of galls by laying eggs in plant tissues, causing the plant to produce abnormal growths. These galls can interfere with the tree''s nutrient flow and overall growth. Termites (Isoptera order) primarily decompose dead wood, moth caterpillars (Lepidoptera order) feed on leaves, and bark beetles (Scolytinae subfamily) bore into bark, but none specifically form galls. Thus, the correct choice is gall wasp.',
   'Gall formation is a key indicator of gall wasp activity.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00056', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-entomology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In the context of forest entomology, what is the primary method for controlling the population of the pine shoot beetle (Tomicus piniperda) in Benguet pine forests?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Chemical pesticides application"},{"id":"b","text":"Use of pheromone traps"},{"id":"c","text":"Biological control using natural predators"},{"id":"d","text":"Mechanical removal of infested shoots"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'Pheromone traps are effective in controlling pine shoot beetle populations by attracting and capturing the beetles, thereby reducing their numbers and impact on the forest.',
   'Pheromone traps work by mimicking the chemical signals used by pine shoot beetles (Tomicus piniperda) to locate mates, effectively luring them into traps where they can be captured and removed. Chemical pesticides (option a) can harm non-target species, biological control (option b) is less established for this pest, and mechanical removal (option c) is labor-intensive and less effective on a large scale. Thus, pheromone traps are the most practical and targeted method.',
   'Remember that pheromone traps are often used for monitoring and controlling insect populations.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00057', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-entomology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'How does the presence of the Ambrosia beetle (Xylosandrus compactus) in a forest ecosystem typically affect the host tree?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Enhances seed dispersal"},{"id":"b","text":"Causes wilting and dieback of branches"},{"id":"c","text":"Facilitates nutrient cycling"},{"id":"d","text":"Improves pollination efficiency"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'The Ambrosia beetle (Xylosandrus compactus) causes wilting and dieback of branches by boring into the wood and introducing fungal pathogens that disrupt the tree''s vascular system.',
   'Ambrosia beetles bore into trees and cultivate fungal gardens, which can block the tree''s water-conducting tissues, leading to wilting and dieback. They do not enhance seed dispersal (option a), facilitate nutrient cycling (option b), or improve pollination efficiency (option d). The correct answer is that they cause wilting and dieback of branches.',
   'Look for symptoms like wilting and dieback when identifying ambrosia beetle damage.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00058', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-entomology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In Integrated Pest Management (IPM) for forest ecosystems, what is the role of pheromones when managing insect populations?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"To attract predators to the forest"},{"id":"b","text":"To confuse insects and reduce mating"},{"id":"c","text":"To enhance insect growth rates"},{"id":"d","text":"To repel insects from tree foliage"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'Pheromones in Integrated Pest Management (IPM) are used to confuse insects, reducing their ability to find mates and thereby decreasing their population over time.',
   'In IPM, pheromones are used to disrupt mating patterns by confusing insects, making it difficult for them to locate each other for reproduction. This method does not attract predators (option a), enhance growth rates (option c), or repel insects (option d). By reducing mating success, the pest population is controlled more sustainably.',
   'Pheromones in IPM are primarily for disrupting mating, not for attracting or repelling.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00059', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-entomology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'Which forest pest is known to cause significant damage by tunneling into the wood and spreading fungal pathogens in Philippine forests?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Scale insect (Coccoidea superfamily)"},{"id":"b","text":"Termite (Isoptera order)"},{"id":"c","text":"Leaf-cutter ant (Atta genus)"},{"id":"d","text":"Ambrosia beetle (Xylosandrus compactus)"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'The Ambrosia beetle (Xylosandrus compactus) tunnels into wood and introduces fungal pathogens, causing significant damage to trees in Philippine forests.',
   'Ambrosia beetles bore into trees and cultivate fungi, which can cause wood decay and structural damage. Scale insects (option a) feed on plant sap, termites (option b) consume wood but do not spread fungi, and leaf-cutter ants (option d) cut leaves for fungal cultivation but do not tunnel into wood. Therefore, the Ambrosia beetle is the correct answer for causing damage by tunneling and spreading fungi.',
   'Ambrosia beetles are known for their symbiotic relationship with fungi, which they introduce into trees.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00060', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-entomology', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'In a study of forest insect dynamics, a researcher is examining the interactions between a bark beetle species and its host trees in a Philippine dipterocarp forest. The researcher notes that the beetle has a symbiotic relationship with a specific fungus that aids in overcoming tree defenses. What is the term for this relationship between the beetle and the fungus?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Mutualism"},{"id":"b","text":"Commensalism"},{"id":"c","text":"Parasitism"},{"id":"d","text":"Predation"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'Mutualism is a type of symbiotic relationship where both organisms involved benefit from the interaction. In this case, the bark beetle benefits from the fungus as it helps in overcoming tree defenses, while the fungus benefits from being transported to new host trees by the beetle.',
   'Mutualism is the correct term because both the beetle and the fungus gain advantages from their relationship. The beetle benefits as the fungus helps it to overcome the tree''s defenses, allowing it to infest the tree more effectively. The fungus benefits by being transported to new trees, which provides it with more resources to thrive. Commensalism (b) is incorrect because it involves one organism benefiting while the other is neither helped nor harmed. Parasitism (c) is incorrect as it involves one organism benefiting at the expense of the other. Predation (d) is incorrect because it involves one organism feeding on another.',
   'Remember that mutualism involves mutual benefits for both parties involved.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00061', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-entomology', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'A forester is trying to estimate the potential population growth of an insect pest in a mahogany plantation. The insect has a reproductive rate of 2.5 and currently, there are 400 insects. Assuming no other limiting factors and using the exponential growth model, what will the population size be after three reproductive cycles?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"4000"},{"id":"b","text":"2500"},{"id":"c","text":"250"},{"id":"d","text":"6250"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'The exponential growth model is used to calculate population growth without limiting factors. The formula is P = P0 * r^t, where P0 is the initial population, r is the growth rate, and t is the number of cycles. Here, P0 = 400, r = 2.5, and t = 3, resulting in a population of 6250.',
   'Using the exponential growth formula P = P0 * r^t, where P0 is the initial population (400), r is the growth rate (2.5), and t is the number of reproductive cycles (3), we calculate: P = 400 * 2.5^3 = 400 * 15.625 = 6250. Option a (4000) is incorrect as it results from a miscalculation of the growth rate or cycles. Option b (2500) is incorrect as it may result from using an incorrect growth rate or cycle count. Option d (250) is incorrect as it represents a decrease rather than growth.',
   'For exponential growth, multiply the initial population by the growth rate raised to the power of the number of cycles.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00062', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-entomology', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'In the context of forest entomology, a forester observes a decline in tree health in a mixed species forest and suspects an insect infestation. Upon examination, the forester finds the presence of scale insects which have a symbiotic relationship with ants. What role do the ants typically play in this interaction?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Herbivores feeding on the scale insects"},{"id":"b","text":"Predators of the scale insects"},{"id":"c","text":"Protectors of the scale insects in exchange for honeydew"},{"id":"d","text":"Competitors for the same host plant resources"}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'In this symbiotic relationship, ants protect scale insects from predators and parasites in exchange for honeydew, a sugary substance produced by the scale insects. This mutualistic interaction benefits both parties involved.',
   'The correct answer is that ants act as protectors of the scale insects in exchange for honeydew. This is a mutualistic relationship where the ants benefit by receiving honeydew, and the scale insects benefit from protection against predators and parasites. Option a (herbivores feeding on the scale insects) is incorrect as ants do not consume the scale insects. Option b (predators of the scale insects) is incorrect because ants do not prey on them. Option d (competitors for the same host plant resources) is incorrect as ants and scale insects do not compete for resources; instead, they cooperate.',
   'Look for interactions where one species provides a service in exchange for a resource, indicating mutualism.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00063', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-pathology', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'What is the primary cause of heart rot in trees?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Bacterial infection"},{"id":"b","text":"Viral infection"},{"id":"c","text":"Fungal infection"},{"id":"d","text":"Insect infestation"}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Heart rot in trees is primarily caused by fungal infections. Fungi invade the heartwood of the tree, leading to decay and structural weakness.',
   'Fungal infection is the correct answer because fungi are the primary organisms responsible for decomposing the lignin and cellulose in the heartwood, leading to heart rot. Bacterial and viral infections do not typically cause heart rot; they affect other parts of the tree or cause different types of diseases. Insect infestation can contribute to the spread of fungi by creating entry points, but insects alone do not cause heart rot. Fungi such as those from the genera Ganoderma and Phellinus are common culprits of heart rot.',
   'Remember: fungi decompose wood, so they cause rot.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00064', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-pathology', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'What is the term for the study of tree diseases and the pathogens that cause them?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Silviculture"},{"id":"b","text":"Dendrology"},{"id":"c","text":"Agroforestry"},{"id":"d","text":"Forest Pathology"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Forest Pathology is the study of tree diseases and the pathogens that cause them. It focuses on understanding and managing diseases that affect forest ecosystems.',
   'Forest Pathology is the correct term because it specifically deals with the study of diseases in trees and their causative agents, including fungi, bacteria, and viruses. Silviculture refers to the practice of managing and cultivating forests, not specifically focusing on diseases. Dendrology is the study of trees and woody plants, focusing on their identification and classification. Agroforestry involves integrating trees and shrubs into agricultural landscapes for environmental and economic benefits, not specifically studying tree diseases.',
   'Pathology always refers to the study of diseases.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00065', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-pathology', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'What is the common name of the disease caused by the pathogen Rigidoporus microporus, which affects rubber trees in the Philippines?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Heart Rot"},{"id":"b","text":"Powdery Mildew"},{"id":"c","text":"White Root Rot"},{"id":"d","text":"Leaf Blight"}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'White Root Rot is the common name for the disease caused by Rigidoporus microporus, which is a significant pathogen affecting rubber trees in the Philippines. This disease primarily attacks the roots, leading to decay and potentially causing the tree to die.',
   'Rigidoporus microporus is a fungal pathogen that specifically causes White Root Rot in rubber trees. This disease is characterized by the decay of the root system, which can lead to the instability and eventual death of the tree. Option b, Heart Rot, is incorrect as it refers to a different type of fungal infection that affects the heartwood of trees, not the roots. Option c, Powdery Mildew, is caused by a different group of fungi and typically affects leaves and stems, not roots. Option d, Leaf Blight, is a general term for diseases that cause leaf damage and is not caused by Rigidoporus microporus.',
   'Remember that root rot diseases often have ''root'' in their name, indicating the part of the plant they affect.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00066', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-pathology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'When assessing a forest stand affected by Armillaria root rot, what is a common symptom to observe in the trees?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Sudden fracturing of the trunk"},{"id":"b","text":"Leaf chlorosis and wilting"},{"id":"c","text":"Basal trunk swelling"},{"id":"d","text":"Formation of witches'' brooms"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'Leaf chlorosis and wilting are common symptoms of Armillaria root rot because the fungus attacks the roots, disrupting water and nutrient uptake, leading to these visible symptoms.',
   'Armillaria root rot primarily affects the root system, causing a reduction in water and nutrient absorption. This results in leaf chlorosis (yellowing) and wilting, as the tree cannot maintain normal physiological functions. Option b, sudden fracturing of the trunk, is more indicative of structural failures rather than a root disease. Option c, basal trunk swelling, is not a typical symptom of Armillaria root rot. Option d, formation of witches'' brooms, is associated with other pathogens like rust fungi or viruses, not Armillaria.',
   'Look for symptoms that indicate root problems, like leaf wilting and yellowing.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00067', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-pathology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'A forester is examining a tree with dieback and yellowing leaves. Which pathogen is most likely responsible for these symptoms in the Philippines?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Phytophthora cinnamomi"},{"id":"b","text":"Fusarium oxysporum"},{"id":"c","text":"Ganoderma lucidum"},{"id":"d","text":"Sphaeropsis sapinea"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'Phytophthora cinnamomi is known to cause dieback and yellowing leaves, particularly in the Philippines, due to its impact on the root system, leading to nutrient deficiencies.',
   'Phytophthora cinnamomi is a soil-borne pathogen that causes root rot, leading to dieback and yellowing leaves as the tree struggles to absorb nutrients. Fusarium oxysporum (option a) typically causes vascular wilts but is less common in trees. Ganoderma lucidum (option b) is associated with root and butt rot but not specifically with leaf symptoms. Sphaeropsis sapinea (option d) causes shoot dieback and cankers, but the symptoms do not match as closely as those caused by Phytophthora.',
   'Associate yellowing leaves and dieback with root pathogens like Phytophthora.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00068', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-pathology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In forest pathology, what is the usual method for controlling the spread of Fusarium wilt in native forest species?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Applying chemical fungicides directly to affected foliage"},{"id":"b","text":"Enhancing soil drainage and aeration"},{"id":"c","text":"Introducing predatory insects to control the fungus"},{"id":"d","text":"Planting resistant tree varieties"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Planting resistant tree varieties is the most effective method to control Fusarium wilt, as it prevents the disease from establishing in the first place.',
   'Fusarium wilt is difficult to control once established, so using resistant varieties is a proactive approach. Chemical fungicides (option a) are often ineffective against soil-borne pathogens like Fusarium. Enhancing soil drainage and aeration (option b) can help but is not a primary control method. Introducing predatory insects (option d) is not applicable to fungal pathogens like Fusarium.',
   'Focus on prevention through resistant varieties for soil-borne diseases.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00069', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-pathology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'What is a typical visual indicator that a tree is infected with the pathogen causing heartwood decay specifically in dipterocarps?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Presence of resin blisters on the bark"},{"id":"b","text":"Yellowing of young shoots"},{"id":"c","text":"Mushroom-like fruiting bodies at the base"},{"id":"d","text":"Excessive leaf litter on the ground"}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Mushroom-like fruiting bodies at the base of a tree are indicative of heartwood decay, as they are the reproductive structures of the fungi causing the decay.',
   'Heartwood decay fungi produce fruiting bodies, often at the base of trees, as they decompose the wood internally. Resin blisters (option a) are more associated with bark beetle infestations. Yellowing of young shoots (option c) is not specific to heartwood decay. Excessive leaf litter (option d) could result from many causes, not specifically heartwood decay.',
   'Look for fungal fruiting bodies as signs of internal decay.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00070', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-pathology', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In diagnosing a fungal infection in a stand of mahogany trees in the Philippines, a forester finds fruiting bodies emerging from the base of several trees. What is the most likely fungal genus causing this symptom?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Phytophthora"},{"id":"b","text":"Pythium"},{"id":"c","text":"Ganoderma"},{"id":"d","text":"Agrobacterium"}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Ganoderma is a common fungal genus known to cause root and butt rot in trees, including mahogany. The presence of fruiting bodies at the base of trees is a typical symptom of Ganoderma infection, as these fungi produce large, shelf-like structures that emerge from the wood.',
   'Ganoderma is the correct choice because it is known for causing root and butt rot in trees, with fruiting bodies often appearing at the base. These fruiting bodies are a key diagnostic feature. Phytophthora and Pythium are both water molds that typically cause root rot but do not produce the large, visible fruiting bodies associated with Ganoderma. Agrobacterium is a bacterium, not a fungus, and it causes crown gall disease, which is characterized by tumor-like growths rather than fruiting bodies. Thus, the presence of fruiting bodies at the base of the trees strongly indicates Ganoderma as the causative agent.',
   'Look for fruiting bodies at the base of trees as a sign of Ganoderma infection.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00071', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-pathology', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'A forester is investigating an outbreak of leaf spots on several native Philippine trees. After laboratory analysis, the leaves show the presence of bacterial ooze on the undersides. Based on this symptom, which genus of bacteria is most likely responsible for the disease?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Phytophthora"},{"id":"b","text":"Armillaria"},{"id":"c","text":"Xanthomonas"},{"id":"d","text":"Rhizoctonia"}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Xanthomonas is a genus of bacteria known to cause leaf spots and is characterized by the presence of bacterial ooze, particularly on the undersides of leaves. This symptom aligns with the laboratory findings in the scenario.',
   'Xanthomonas is a bacterial pathogen that often causes leaf spots with a characteristic bacterial ooze. Phytophthora, Armillaria, and Rhizoctonia are not bacteria; Phytophthora is an oomycete, while Armillaria and Rhizoctonia are fungi, which do not produce bacterial ooze. Thus, the presence of bacterial ooze specifically points to Xanthomonas.',
   'Bacterial ooze on leaves often indicates Xanthomonas.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00072', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-pathology', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'A tree exhibits symptoms such as cankers on the bark, dieback of branches, and stunted growth. Upon closer inspection, hyphae and conidiophores are visible. Which of the following fungal genera is most likely responsible for the infection?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Botryosphaeria"},{"id":"b","text":"Pythium"},{"id":"c","text":"Ganoderma"},{"id":"d","text":"Verticillium"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'Botryosphaeria is a fungal genus known for causing cankers, dieback, and stunted growth, with visible hyphae and conidiophores, which are consistent with the symptoms described.',
   'Botryosphaeria causes cankers and dieback, with visible hyphae and conidiophores. Pythium is an oomycete, not a true fungus, and typically affects roots. Ganoderma causes root and butt rot, not cankers and dieback. Verticillium causes wilt, not cankers or dieback. Thus, the symptoms match Botryosphaeria infections.',
   'Cankers and dieback with visible fungal structures suggest Botryosphaeria.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00073', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-pathology', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'In a plantation of mahogany trees, a forester notices chlorosis and wilting in several young trees. Soil analysis reveals waterlogged conditions with poor drainage. Which pathogen is most likely exacerbating the trees'' stress due to these conditions?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Fusarium oxysporum"},{"id":"b","text":"Phytophthora cinnamomi"},{"id":"c","text":"Ceratocystis fimbriata"},{"id":"d","text":"Sphaeropsis sapinea"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'Phytophthora cinnamomi thrives in waterlogged conditions and is known to cause root rot, leading to symptoms like chlorosis and wilting in trees, particularly under poor drainage.',
   'Phytophthora cinnamomi is an oomycete that causes root rot in waterlogged soils, leading to chlorosis and wilting. Fusarium oxysporum causes vascular wilt but is less associated with waterlogged conditions. Ceratocystis fimbriata causes wilt but is linked to wounds and not specifically waterlogged conditions. Sphaeropsis sapinea primarily affects conifers, not mahogany. Thus, Phytophthora cinnamomi is the most likely pathogen in this scenario.',
   'Chlorosis and wilting in waterlogged soils often indicate Phytophthora.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00074', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-biodiversity', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'What is the primary role of mangroves in coastal forest ecosystems in the Philippines?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Provide habitat for terrestrial mammals."},{"id":"b","text":"Serve as nurseries for marine life."},{"id":"c","text":"Prevent saltwater intrusion into groundwater."},{"id":"d","text":"Reduce the risk of forest fires."}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'Mangroves play a crucial role in coastal ecosystems by providing a safe and nutrient-rich environment for the juvenile stages of many marine species, including fish and crustaceans. This function supports marine biodiversity and fisheries.',
   'Option c is correct because mangroves serve as nurseries for marine life, offering protection and food for young marine organisms. Option a is incorrect as mangroves are more associated with aquatic and semi-aquatic life rather than terrestrial mammals. Option b is incorrect because while mangroves can help in reducing saltwater intrusion, their primary ecological role is not this. Option d is incorrect as mangroves are not primarily involved in reducing forest fires, which are more relevant to terrestrial forest ecosystems.',
   'Look for keywords like ''nurseries'' and ''marine life'' when identifying the role of mangroves.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00075', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-biodiversity', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'Which law in the Philippines provides the legal framework for the establishment and management of protected areas to conserve biodiversity?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Republic Act No. 7586 (NIPAS Act)"},{"id":"b","text":"Republic Act No. 8371 (Indigenous Peoples'' Rights Act)"},{"id":"c","text":"Presidential Decree No. 705 (Revised Forestry Code)"},{"id":"d","text":"Republic Act No. 9147 (Wildlife Resources Conservation and Protection Act)"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'Republic Act No. 7586, known as the National Integrated Protected Areas System (NIPAS) Act, establishes the legal framework for the creation and management of protected areas in the Philippines to conserve biodiversity.',
   'Option b is correct because RA 7586 (NIPAS Act) is specifically designed to establish and manage protected areas for biodiversity conservation. Option a is incorrect as RA 8371 focuses on the rights of indigenous peoples. Option c is incorrect because PD 705 is concerned with forestry laws and regulations. Option d is incorrect as RA 9147 deals with the conservation and protection of wildlife resources, not specifically the establishment of protected areas.',
   'Remember that NIPAS Act is synonymous with protected areas and biodiversity conservation.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00076', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-biodiversity', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'In the Philippine forest ecosystem, which of the following is a key characteristic of dipterocarp forests?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Dominance by coniferous species."},{"id":"b","text":"Presence of limestone outcrops."},{"id":"c","text":"Abundance of deciduous trees that shed leaves seasonally."},{"id":"d","text":"Canopy made up of tall, large-leaved trees."}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Dipterocarp forests are characterized by a canopy of tall, large-leaved trees, which are typical of this forest type in the Philippines. These trees dominate the landscape and create a dense canopy.',
   'Option c is correct because dipterocarp forests are known for their tall, large-leaved trees forming a dense canopy. Option a is incorrect as coniferous species are not dominant in dipterocarp forests. Option b is incorrect because limestone outcrops are not a defining feature of dipterocarp forests. Option d is incorrect as dipterocarp forests are not characterized by deciduous trees; they are primarily evergreen.',
   'Focus on the height and leaf size of trees when identifying dipterocarp forests.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00077', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-biodiversity', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In a forest ecosystem, what is the biodiversity significance of the understory?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"It provides shade for seedlings."},{"id":"b","text":"It is typically dominated by large canopy trees."},{"id":"c","text":"It is composed mainly of dead organic matter."},{"id":"d","text":"It supports plant species adapted to low light conditions."}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'The understory of a forest is crucial for supporting plant species that thrive in low light conditions, as it is located beneath the canopy where sunlight is limited. These plants have adapted to survive with less light, contributing to the overall biodiversity of the forest ecosystem.',
   'Option b is correct because the understory is an environment where light is filtered through the canopy, allowing only species adapted to low light to thrive. Option a is incorrect because while the understory does provide some shade, its primary significance is supporting low-light species. Option c is incorrect because large canopy trees dominate the canopy layer, not the understory. Option d is incorrect because while dead organic matter can be present, it is not the main component of the understory.',
   'Remember that the understory is characterized by low light conditions, which is key to its biodiversity significance.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00078', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-biodiversity', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'How does increased species diversity in a forest ecosystem contribute to its resilience?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"By relying on a single species for ecosystem functions."},{"id":"b","text":"By increasing the likelihood of species extinctions."},{"id":"c","text":"By providing a variety of responses to environmental changes."},{"id":"d","text":"By reducing the structural complexity of the forest."}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Increased species diversity enhances a forest ecosystem''s resilience by ensuring that there are multiple species that can respond differently to environmental changes, thus maintaining ecosystem functions under various conditions.',
   'Option c is correct because a diverse array of species provides a variety of responses to changes, such as climate shifts or pest invasions, enhancing resilience. Option a is incorrect because relying on a single species makes the ecosystem vulnerable. Option b is incorrect as increased diversity generally reduces the likelihood of extinctions by providing more ecological niches. Option d is incorrect because diversity increases, not reduces, structural complexity.',
   'Think of diversity as a safety net that allows ecosystems to adapt to changes.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00079', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-biodiversity', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'Which term describes the introduction of non-native species into a forest ecosystem, potentially threatening local biodiversity?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Biological invasion"},{"id":"b","text":"Natural succession"},{"id":"c","text":"Endemism"},{"id":"d","text":"Co-evolution"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'Biological invasion refers to the introduction of non-native species into an ecosystem, which can disrupt local biodiversity by outcompeting native species and altering habitat conditions.',
   'Option a is correct because biological invasion involves non-native species that can threaten local ecosystems. Option b is incorrect because natural succession is a natural process of ecological change. Option c is incorrect because endemism refers to species native to a specific location. Option d is incorrect because co-evolution involves mutual evolutionary influence between interacting species.',
   'Non-native species introductions are often termed biological invasions.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00080', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-biodiversity', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In the context of forest biodiversity, what is the role of keystone species?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"They contribute to the forest''s genetic diversity by interbreeding with multiple species."},{"id":"b","text":"They are common throughout the forest and dominate in numbers."},{"id":"c","text":"They are species that migrate seasonally through the forest ecosystem."},{"id":"d","text":"They have a disproportionate effect on the structure and function of their ecosystem."}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Keystone species play a critical role in maintaining the structure and function of their ecosystem, often having effects that are disproportionate to their abundance.',
   'Option b is correct because keystone species have a significant impact on their ecosystem, influencing various ecological processes. Option a is incorrect because keystone species do not necessarily interbreed with multiple species. Option c is incorrect because keystone species are not defined by their abundance. Option d is incorrect because keystone species are not defined by migration patterns.',
   'Keystone species are crucial for ecosystem stability and function, often with effects beyond their numbers.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00081', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-biodiversity', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In the biodiversity of the Philippine forest ecosystem, how does the presence of dipterocarp species influence the forest structure?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"They solely support understory plant growth, reducing canopy complexity."},{"id":"b","text":"They decrease biodiversity by outcompeting all other tree species."},{"id":"c","text":"They are absent in lowland forest ecosystems, contributing to homogeneity."},{"id":"d","text":"They provide a multilayered canopy which enhances habitat diversity."}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Dipterocarp species are known for their tall stature and broad crowns, which contribute to a multilayered canopy structure. This complex canopy provides various niches and microhabitats, supporting a diverse range of flora and fauna.',
   'The correct answer is ''a'' because dipterocarp trees, which are prevalent in the Philippine forests, form a multilayered canopy. This structure is crucial for enhancing habitat diversity as it creates different layers of vegetation, each supporting different species of plants and animals. Option ''b'' is incorrect because dipterocarps do not solely support understory growth; they contribute to overall forest complexity. Option ''c'' is incorrect as dipterocarps do not decrease biodiversity; they actually support it by providing diverse habitats. Option ''d'' is incorrect because dipterocarps are a significant component of lowland forests, contributing to their biodiversity rather than homogeneity.',
   'Remember that dipterocarps are key to creating complex forest structures, which support high biodiversity.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00082', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-biodiversity', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'In a forest ecosystem, the Shannon diversity index (H'') is often used to quantify biodiversity. Given a forest plot with the following species abundance data: 10 individuals of species A, 15 individuals of species B, and 5 individuals of species C, calculate the Shannon diversity index. Use the formula: H'' = -Σ (pi * ln(pi)), where pi is the proportion of individuals of species i in the total population.',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"0.936"},{"id":"b","text":"1.011"},{"id":"c","text":"1.098"},{"id":"d","text":"1.204"}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'The Shannon diversity index (H'') is calculated using the formula H'' = -Σ (pi * ln(pi)), where pi is the proportion of individuals of each species in the total population. For this forest plot, the proportions are calculated for each species, and the resulting values are summed to find the index. The correct calculation results in an index of 0.936.',
   'To calculate the Shannon diversity index, first determine the total number of individuals: 10 (species A) + 15 (species B) + 5 (species C) = 30 individuals. The proportions (pi) are: species A = 10/30 = 0.333, species B = 15/30 = 0.5, species C = 5/30 = 0.167. Calculate each term: for A, -0.333 * ln(0.333) = 0.366; for B, -0.5 * ln(0.5) = 0.347; for C, -0.167 * ln(0.167) = 0.223. Summing these gives 0.936. Other options result from incorrect calculations: a. 1.011, c. 1.098, and d. 1.204 do not match the correct sum of the terms.',
   'Remember that the Shannon index involves calculating the proportion of each species, multiplying by the natural log of that proportion, and summing these values.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00083', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-biodiversity', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'In the Philippine forest ecosystem, mycorrhizal fungi play a crucial role in supporting biodiversity. Which of the following best describes the relationship between mycorrhizal fungi and the trees they associate with?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Parasitism, where the fungi exploit the host trees for nutrients."},{"id":"b","text":"Commensalism, where the fungi benefit without affecting the host trees."},{"id":"c","text":"Mutualism, where both the fungi and the host trees benefit from each other."},{"id":"d","text":"Competition, where the fungi and the host trees compete for soil resources."}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Mycorrhizal fungi and trees engage in a mutualistic relationship where both parties benefit. The fungi enhance nutrient and water absorption for the trees, while the trees provide carbohydrates to the fungi.',
   'In mutualism, both organisms benefit from the relationship. Mycorrhizal fungi extend the root system of trees, increasing nutrient and water uptake, while receiving carbohydrates produced by the tree through photosynthesis. Option a (parasitism) is incorrect because the fungi do not exploit the trees; they provide benefits. Option b (commensalism) is incorrect because the trees are positively affected. Option d (competition) is incorrect because the fungi and trees do not compete; they cooperate.',
   'Mutualism involves a reciprocal benefit, often seen in nutrient exchange relationships like that of mycorrhizal fungi and trees.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00084', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-biodiversity', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'Within the context of the Philippine forest ecosystem, the concept of ''alpha diversity'' refers to which of the following?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"The total diversity of ecosystems within a landscape."},{"id":"b","text":"The genetic variation within a single species across different areas."},{"id":"c","text":"The diversity of species within a specific area or ecosystem."},{"id":"d","text":"The overall diversity considering both ecosystem and landscape levels."}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Alpha diversity refers to the diversity of species within a specific area or ecosystem, focusing on the number of species present in a localized habitat.',
   'Alpha diversity measures the variety of species in a specific area or ecosystem, providing insight into the richness of that habitat. Option a describes beta diversity, which looks at diversity between ecosystems. Option c refers to genetic diversity within a species, not species diversity in an area. Option d describes gamma diversity, which considers diversity at larger scales, including ecosystem and landscape levels.',
   'Alpha diversity is about species richness in a specific, localized area, often measured by counting species.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00085', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-genetics-tree-improvement', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'What is the primary purpose of a seed orchard in forest tree improvement?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"To serve as a natural wildlife habitat."},{"id":"b","text":"To conduct ecological research."},{"id":"c","text":"To produce genetically improved seeds for reforestation."},{"id":"d","text":"To produce timber for commercial purposes."}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'The primary purpose of a seed orchard is to produce genetically improved seeds that are used for reforestation efforts. These orchards are specifically designed and managed to enhance the genetic quality of seeds, which leads to better growth, disease resistance, and adaptability in reforested areas.',
   'Option a is correct because seed orchards are established to produce seeds with superior genetic traits, which are crucial for successful reforestation projects. Option b is incorrect because while seed orchards may incidentally provide habitat for wildlife, this is not their primary purpose. Option c is incorrect as ecological research is not the main function of a seed orchard, although research may be conducted there. Option d is incorrect because seed orchards are not primarily intended for timber production; they focus on seed production.',
   'Remember that seed orchards are about seeds, not timber or research.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00086', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-genetics-tree-improvement', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'In the context of forest genetics, what is the definition of ''progeny test''?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"A test to determine soil composition in a forest area."},{"id":"b","text":"A test to measure the growth rate of mature trees."},{"id":"c","text":"A test to analyze the water content of tree leaves."},{"id":"d","text":"A test to evaluate the performance of offspring from specific parent trees."}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'A progeny test in forest genetics is designed to evaluate the performance of offspring from specific parent trees. This helps in assessing the genetic quality and potential of the parent trees based on the traits exhibited by their progeny.',
   'Option a is correct because progeny tests involve growing offspring from selected parent trees to evaluate their performance in terms of growth, disease resistance, and other desirable traits. Option b is incorrect because soil composition tests are unrelated to progeny testing. Option c is incorrect as progeny tests focus on young trees or seedlings, not mature trees. Option d is incorrect because water content analysis of leaves is not part of progeny testing, which is concerned with genetic evaluation.',
   'Link ''progeny'' with ''offspring'' to remember its focus on evaluating young trees.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00087', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-genetics-tree-improvement', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'What is the primary objective of tree improvement in forest genetics?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"To enhance growth rates, yield, and adaptability of tree species."},{"id":"b","text":"To increase the genetic diversity of tree species."},{"id":"c","text":"To reduce the carbon footprint of forest operations."},{"id":"d","text":"To restore natural forest ecosystems to their original state."}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'The primary objective of tree improvement in forest genetics is to enhance growth rates, yield, and adaptability of tree species. This involves selecting and breeding trees with desirable traits to improve their performance in specific environments, ultimately leading to more productive and resilient forests.',
   'Option b is correct because tree improvement focuses on selecting and breeding trees that exhibit superior growth rates, higher yields, and better adaptability to environmental conditions. This is achieved through genetic selection and breeding programs. Option a is incorrect because while genetic diversity is important, the main goal of tree improvement is not to increase diversity but to enhance specific traits. Option c is incorrect because reducing the carbon footprint is not a direct objective of tree improvement, although improved tree growth can contribute to carbon sequestration. Option d is incorrect because restoring natural ecosystems is more related to conservation efforts rather than tree improvement, which focuses on enhancing specific traits for productivity and adaptability.',
   'Remember that tree improvement is about enhancing specific traits like growth and yield, not just maintaining genetic diversity.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00088', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-genetics-tree-improvement', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'When selecting seed sources for a reforestation project in the Philippines, which factor is most crucial to ensure adaptability and growth performance in the selected area?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"The genetic variability of the seed source."},{"id":"b","text":"The moisture content of the seeds."},{"id":"c","text":"The distance of the seed source from the planting site."},{"id":"d","text":"The elevation of the seed source relative to the planting site."}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'The elevation of the seed source relative to the planting site is crucial because it affects the climate and environmental conditions that the seeds are adapted to. Seeds from a similar elevation are more likely to thrive in the target area due to similar temperature, humidity, and other climatic factors.',
   'The elevation of the seed source (option b) is crucial because it directly influences the climatic conditions to which the seeds are adapted. Seeds from a similar elevation to the planting site are more likely to have the necessary adaptations for survival and growth. Option a, genetic variability, while important, does not directly address environmental adaptation. Option c, moisture content, is important for seed storage but not for adaptation. Option d, distance, affects logistics but not adaptability.',
   'Match seed source elevation to planting site elevation for best adaptability.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00089', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-genetics-tree-improvement', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In a tree improvement program, which method involves selecting individuals based on their phenotypic superiority and using them as parents for the next generation?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Recurrent selection"},{"id":"b","text":"Mass selection"},{"id":"c","text":"Family selection"},{"id":"d","text":"Clonal selection"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'Mass selection involves choosing individuals based on observable traits, or phenotypes, and using them as parents for the next generation. This method is straightforward and effective for improving traits that are easily measured.',
   'Mass selection (option a) is the correct method as it involves selecting individuals based on their phenotypic superiority, making it suitable for traits that are easily observed and measured. Option b, recurrent selection, involves repeated cycles of selection and breeding, which is more complex. Option c, family selection, focuses on selecting entire families rather than individuals. Option d, clonal selection, involves selecting and propagating clones, not individuals based on phenotype.',
   'Look for ''phenotypic superiority'' to identify mass selection.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00090', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-genetics-tree-improvement', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'Which term describes the genetic structure of a tree population that results from non-random mating due to geographical or ecological isolation?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Gene flow"},{"id":"b","text":"Genetic drift"},{"id":"c","text":"Population subdivision"},{"id":"d","text":"Genetic bottleneck"}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'Population subdivision refers to the genetic structure that arises from non-random mating due to geographical or ecological isolation, leading to distinct subpopulations within a species.',
   'Population subdivision (option d) is the correct term as it describes the genetic structure resulting from non-random mating due to isolation. Option a, gene flow, involves the transfer of genes between populations, which counters isolation. Option b, genetic drift, refers to random changes in allele frequencies. Option c, genetic bottleneck, describes a reduction in genetic diversity due to a significant population size reduction.',
   'Non-random mating due to isolation points to population subdivision.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00091', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-genetics-tree-improvement', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In the context of forest genetics, what technique is used to determine the genetic diversity within a population by examining the DNA sequences of specific genes?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Provenance trial"},{"id":"b","text":"Hybrid vigor assessment"},{"id":"c","text":"Phenotypic selection"},{"id":"d","text":"Molecular marker analysis"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Molecular marker analysis is used to assess genetic diversity by examining DNA sequences, providing detailed information about genetic variation within a population.',
   'Molecular marker analysis (option b) is the correct technique as it involves examining DNA sequences to determine genetic diversity. Option a, provenance trial, tests different seed sources for adaptability. Option c, hybrid vigor assessment, evaluates the performance of hybrids. Option d, phenotypic selection, involves selecting individuals based on observable traits, not genetic analysis.',
   'DNA sequence examination indicates molecular marker analysis.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00092', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-genetics-tree-improvement', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'When conducting a progeny test in a tree improvement program, what is the primary variable being tested to evaluate the genetic potential of the parent trees?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"The seed viability of the progeny"},{"id":"b","text":"The flowering time of the progeny"},{"id":"c","text":"The root depth of the progeny"},{"id":"d","text":"The growth rate of the progeny"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'The growth rate of the progeny is the primary variable tested in progeny tests to evaluate the genetic potential of parent trees, as it directly reflects the inherited traits that affect growth.',
   'The growth rate of the progeny (option a) is the primary variable in progeny tests because it directly indicates the genetic potential for growth traits inherited from the parents. Option b, seed viability, is more about seed quality than genetic potential. Option c, flowering time, is a specific trait not typically the focus of progeny tests. Option d, root depth, while important, is not as commonly measured as growth rate in these tests.',
   'Focus on growth rate as the key indicator in progeny tests.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fe-g00093', 'forest-ecosystem', 'forest-genetics-tree-improvement', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'In the context of forest genetics, a forester is tasked with improving the growth rate of Swietenia macrophylla (mahogany) by selecting for a specific genetic trait. The forester decides to use a backcross breeding strategy to enhance a desirable trait found in a particular mahogany cultivar while retaining the superior growth characteristics of a local population. Which of the following best describes the backcross breeding strategy in this context?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Crossing the selected cultivar with another unrelated species to introduce genetic diversity, followed by multiple generations of selection."},{"id":"b","text":"Crossing the selected cultivar with the local population, then backcrossing the offspring with the selected cultivar to enhance the desirable trait."},{"id":"c","text":"Crossing the selected cultivar with the local population, then backcrossing the offspring with the local population to reinforce the desirable trait."},{"id":"d","text":"Crossing the selected cultivar with its own offspring to rapidly fix the trait within a few generations."}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'Option c is correct because a backcross breeding strategy involves crossing the offspring back to one of the original parents to enhance a specific trait. In this context, the offspring are backcrossed with the selected cultivar to enhance the desirable trait while maintaining the growth characteristics of the local population.',
   'In a backcross breeding strategy, the goal is to enhance a specific trait by repeatedly crossing offspring with one of the original parents. Option c correctly describes this approach by crossing the selected cultivar with the local population to produce offspring, and then backcrossing these offspring with the selected cultivar. This reinforces the desirable trait from the selected cultivar. Option a is incorrect because it introduces an unrelated species, which is not part of a backcross strategy. Option b is incorrect because it backcrosses with the local population, which would dilute the desirable trait rather than enhance it. Option d is incorrect because crossing with its own offspring does not constitute a backcross and can lead to inbreeding.',
   'Remember that backcrossing involves returning to one of the original parents to reinforce a trait.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fg-g00001', 'forest-governance', 'forest-history', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'Which law in the Philippines is known as the National Integrated Protected Areas System Act of 1992?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"PD 705"},{"id":"b","text":"RA 9147"},{"id":"c","text":"RA 8371"},{"id":"d","text":"RA 7586"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'RA 7586 is the National Integrated Protected Areas System (NIPAS) Act of 1992, which establishes a comprehensive system of protected areas in the Philippines to conserve biodiversity and natural resources.',
   'RA 7586, known as the NIPAS Act of 1992, is specifically designed to protect and manage significant natural areas in the Philippines. It aims to secure the perpetual existence of all native plants and animals through the establishment of a comprehensive system of integrated protected areas. PD 705, the Forestry Reform Code, focuses on forest management and conservation, not specifically on protected areas. RA 9147 is the Wildlife Resources Conservation and Protection Act, which deals with wildlife conservation, not protected areas. RA 8371 is the Indigenous Peoples'' Rights Act, focusing on the rights of indigenous communities, not protected areas.',
   'Remember: RA 7586 = NIPAS Act, focusing on protected areas.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fg-g00002', 'forest-governance', 'forest-history', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'What is the main focus of Presidential Decree No. 705?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Wildlife Conservation Act"},{"id":"b","text":"Forestry Reform Code of the Philippines"},{"id":"c","text":"Indigenous Peoples'' Rights Act"},{"id":"d","text":"Environmental Awareness and Education Act"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'Presidential Decree No. 705 is known as the Forestry Reform Code of the Philippines, which provides for the sustainable management and utilization of forest resources.',
   'PD 705, the Forestry Reform Code, was enacted to promote the sustainable management and development of the country''s forest resources. It includes provisions for forest protection, reforestation, and forest land use. The other options are unrelated: the Wildlife Conservation Act refers to RA 9147, the Indigenous Peoples'' Rights Act is RA 8371, and the Environmental Awareness and Education Act is RA 9512, none of which are related to the Forestry Reform Code.',
   'PD 705 is synonymous with forestry reform in the Philippines.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fg-g00003', 'forest-governance', 'forest-history', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'The Indigenous Peoples'' Rights Act is also known by which Republic Act number?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"RA 9147"},{"id":"b","text":"RA 11038"},{"id":"c","text":"RA 8371"},{"id":"d","text":"RA 7586"}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'RA 8371 is the Indigenous Peoples'' Rights Act, which recognizes and promotes the rights of indigenous cultural communities in the Philippines.',
   'RA 8371, known as the Indigenous Peoples'' Rights Act, focuses on recognizing, protecting, and promoting the rights of indigenous peoples in the Philippines. It includes provisions for ancestral domain, self-governance, and cultural integrity. RA 9147 is about wildlife conservation, RA 11038 pertains to the expansion of protected areas, and RA 7586 is the NIPAS Act, none of which address indigenous peoples'' rights.',
   'RA 8371 is key for indigenous rights; remember it as the Indigenous Peoples'' Rights Act.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fg-g00004', 'forest-governance', 'forest-history', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'Which Act provides for the conservation and protection of wildlife resources and their habitats in the Philippines?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"RA 8371"},{"id":"b","text":"RA 9147"},{"id":"c","text":"RA 7586"},{"id":"d","text":"PD 705"}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'RA 9147 is the Wildlife Resources Conservation and Protection Act, which aims to conserve and protect wildlife resources and their habitats in the Philippines.',
   'RA 9147, the Wildlife Resources Conservation and Protection Act, focuses on the conservation and protection of wildlife and their habitats. It includes measures for regulating the collection and trade of wildlife. RA 8371 is about indigenous peoples'' rights, RA 7586 is the NIPAS Act concerning protected areas, and PD 705 is the Forestry Reform Code, none of which specifically address wildlife conservation.',
   'RA 9147 is your go-to for wildlife conservation laws.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fg-g00005', 'forest-governance', 'forest-history', 'Easy', 'practice',
   'What is the primary objective of Republic Act No. 11038?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Rehabilitation of Mangroves"},{"id":"b","text":"Reforestation of the Cordillera Region"},{"id":"c","text":"Establishment of Community-Based Forest Management Areas"},{"id":"d","text":"Expansion of the NIPAS"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'Republic Act No. 11038 is the Expanded National Integrated Protected Areas System (E-NIPAS) Act of 2018, which aims to enhance the protection of the country''s protected areas.',
   'RA 11038, the E-NIPAS Act, expands and strengthens the protection of the country''s protected areas by adding more sites to the NIPAS and enhancing management strategies. It builds on the original NIPAS Act (RA 7586). The other options are unrelated: mangrove rehabilitation, Cordillera reforestation, and community-based forest management are not the primary focus of RA 11038.',
   'RA 11038 expands NIPAS; think of it as NIPAS 2.0.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fg-g00006', 'forest-governance', 'forest-history', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In the context of Philippine forest history, what was the primary purpose of the Kaingin Management and Land Settlement Act of 1964?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"To promote sustainable tourism in forested areas."},{"id":"b","text":"To allocate forest lands for commercial logging."},{"id":"c","text":"To establish national parks throughout the Philippines."},{"id":"d","text":"To regulate and manage kaingin (slash-and-burn farming) activities."}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'The Kaingin Management and Land Settlement Act of 1964 was specifically enacted to address the widespread practice of kaingin, or slash-and-burn farming, which was leading to deforestation and land degradation. By regulating these activities, the Act aimed to manage and mitigate the environmental impact of such practices.',
   'Option b is correct because the Kaingin Management and Land Settlement Act of 1964 was designed to control and manage the practice of kaingin, which involves clearing forest land by burning for agricultural purposes. This Act sought to prevent the destructive effects of slash-and-burn farming on the environment. Option a is incorrect because the Act was not related to tourism. Option c is incorrect as it did not focus on commercial logging. Option d is incorrect because the Act did not establish national parks.',
   'Remember: ''Kaingin'' refers to slash-and-burn farming, so any law with ''Kaingin'' likely addresses this practice.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fg-g00007', 'forest-governance', 'forest-history', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'The establishment of the Forest Management Bureau (FMB) in 1987 was primarily focused on what aspect of forestry in the Philippines?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Regulating mining activities within forest reserves."},{"id":"b","text":"Streamlining the issuance of logging permits."},{"id":"c","text":"Enhancing community-based forest management programs."},{"id":"d","text":"Developing urban forests in metropolitan areas."}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'The Forest Management Bureau (FMB) was established to enhance the management of forest resources, with a strong focus on community involvement. This aligns with the goal of enhancing community-based forest management programs.',
   'Option b is correct because the FMB''s primary focus was on improving forest management through community-based programs, which involve local communities in the sustainable management of forests. Option a is incorrect because the FMB does not primarily regulate mining activities. Option c is incorrect as the FMB''s main role is not to streamline logging permits. Option d is incorrect because the FMB does not focus on urban forests.',
   'Think ''community'' when you see ''Forest Management Bureau'' and ''1987''.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fg-g00008', 'forest-governance', 'forest-history', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'In Philippine forest history, which initiative was launched to involve local communities in the sustainable management of forest resources?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Integrated Area Development Program (IADP)"},{"id":"b","text":"National Greening Program (NGP)"},{"id":"c","text":"Industrial Tree Plantation Program (ITPP)"},{"id":"d","text":"Community-Based Forest Management Program (CBFMP)"}]'::jsonb, 'd',
   'The Community-Based Forest Management Program (CBFMP) was specifically designed to involve local communities in the sustainable management and conservation of forest resources, making it the correct initiative.',
   'Option a is correct because the CBFMP was launched to engage local communities in managing forest resources sustainably. Option b, the Integrated Area Development Program, is broader and not specific to community forest management. Option c, the National Greening Program, focuses on reforestation rather than community involvement. Option d, the Industrial Tree Plantation Program, is more about commercial tree farming than community management.',
   '''Community-Based'' in the name directly indicates local community involvement.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
  ('fg-g00009', 'forest-governance', 'forest-history', 'Medium', 'practice',
   'What was a significant impact of the Revised Forestry Code of the Philippines on forest land allocation?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"It restricted private ownership of forest lands."},{"id":"b","text":"It increased the conversion of forest lands into agricultural areas."},{"id":"c","text":"It prioritized urban development over forest conservation."},{"id":"d","text":"It established a system for sustainable logging practices."}]'::jsonb, 'a',
   'The Revised Forestry Code of the Philippines restricted private ownership of forest lands to ensure that these lands were managed sustainably and remained under government control, preventing their conversion to other uses.',
   'Option d is correct because the Revised Forestry Code restricted private ownership to maintain government oversight and sustainable management of forest lands. Option a is incorrect as the Code aimed to prevent the conversion of forest lands to agriculture. Option b is incorrect because it did not prioritize urban development. Option c is incorrect as it did not primarily establish sustainable logging practices.',
   '''Revised Forestry Code'' often means more government control, less private ownership.',
