   '[{"id":"a","text":"By allowing them to veto any forestry project on their ancestral lands regardless of its nature."},{"id":"b","text":"By ensuring their participation and approval is sought before any project affecting their lands can proceed."},{"id":"c","text":"By granting them exclusive rights to exploit forest resources without government oversight."},{"id":"d","text":"By providing them financial compensation for any project implemented on their lands."}]'::jsonb, 'b',
   'The Free, Prior, and Informed Consent (FPIC) process under the Indigenous Peoples'' Rights Act (RA 8371) ensures that indigenous communities are actively involved in decisions affecting their ancestral lands. It mandates that their consent must be obtained before any project can proceed, thereby empowering them in forest governance.',
   'Option b is correct because the FPIC process, as outlined in RA 8371, requires that indigenous peoples are not only informed about projects affecting their lands but also that their consent is obtained before such projects can proceed. This ensures that they have a say in the management and use of their ancestral domains. Option a is incorrect because FPIC does not grant an absolute veto power; it requires a process of negotiation and agreement. Option c is incorrect because while indigenous peoples have rights to their lands, these rights are not without government oversight, especially in terms of sustainable management. Option d is incorrect because FPIC is about consent and participation, not merely financial compensation.',
   'Remember that FPIC is about participation and consent, not just financial compensation or exclusive rights.',
