  ('fg-g00027', 'forest-governance', 'policy-and-administration', 'Hard', 'practice',
   'In the context of the National Integrated Protected Areas System (NIPAS) as amended by RA 11038, which of the following statements accurately distinguishes between a ''Protected Area'' and a ''Buffer Zone''?',
   '[{"id":"a","text":"Protected Areas are zones where strict protection is enforced, while Buffer Zones allow any kind of resource use without restrictions."},{"id":"b","text":"Protected Areas are designated mainly for tourism, while Buffer Zones are designated exclusively for scientific research."},{"id":"c","text":"Protected Areas have clearly demarcated boundaries for conservation, while Buffer Zones are transition areas where limited sustainable activities are permitted."},{"id":"d","text":"Protected Areas are managed by the national government, while Buffer Zones are managed solely by local government units."}]'::jsonb, 'c',
   'RA 11038 distinguishes Protected Areas as zones with strict conservation measures and clearly defined boundaries, while Buffer Zones are transitional areas where some sustainable use is allowed to mitigate impacts on the Protected Area.',
   'Option b is correct because it accurately reflects the distinction made in RA 11038, where Protected Areas have strict conservation measures and clear boundaries, while Buffer Zones allow for limited sustainable use to reduce pressure on the Protected Area. Option a is incorrect because Buffer Zones do not allow unrestricted resource use; they have controlled activities. Option c is incorrect because Protected Areas are not designated mainly for tourism, and Buffer Zones are not exclusively for research. Option d is incorrect because both Protected Areas and Buffer Zones involve management roles for both national and local government units, not exclusively one or the other.',
   'Protected Areas have strict boundaries; Buffer Zones allow some sustainable use.',
   now(), 'gpt-4o'),
